<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khilafat movement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a>–<a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Khilafat movement was a political campaign of Muslims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> against British policy towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and the planned dismemberment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. Its leaders, among them Abul Kalam Azad and the brothers Mohammad Ali Jauhar and Shaukat Ali, formed the All <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> Khilafat Committee to protect the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>. In 1920 they allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a>, and the Khilafatists became a major part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/non-cooperation-movement">Non-cooperation movement</a>. Noted for promoting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>–Muslim unity, it faded after that campaign was called off in 1922, and Turkey's abolition of the caliphate in 1924 ended its cause.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khilafat-movement.jpg" alt="Khilafat movement" width="250" height="170" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khilafat_activists_leading_a_procession.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
